## What is configurable vs static

### Configurable per tenant (no deploy)

| Config | Where |
|---|---|
| Business profile, intro, knowledge | `Tenant`, `Agent.knowledgeText` |
| Flow graph (within stage types) | `Agent.flow` JSON |
| Catalog display mode | `inbox` / `faq` |
| Enabled capabilities | talk stage `capabilities[]` |
| Booking fields to collect | `required_for_book` on the talk stage |
| Venue hours, booking templates, stay collect list, nouns | `CapabilityInstance.config` |
| Lead schema keys | `Agent.leadSchema` |
| HITL policy | `Agent.hitlPolicy` |
| Restart policy, nudges | Flow JSON |
| Language | `Tenant.chatLanguage` + copy files (mechanics) |
| Channel bindings | `ChannelConnection` |

### Static (requires code deploy)

| Piece | Why |
|---|---|
| Stage types (`talk`, `classify`, `collect`, `faq`, `action`, `terminal`) | Interpreter switch in `interpreter.ts` |
| Field *types* (`date`, `date_range`, `datetime_text`, `enum`, …) | `src/lib/flow/fields/` |
| Channel adapters (Zernio, HookMyApp) | `channels/` |
| Request persistence + HITL | `src/lib/requests.ts` |
| Copy/i18n sentence mechanics | `src/lib/copy/` |
| Validation rules | `validate.ts` |

Adding a **new business type** (barber, villa, dress rental) is a `CapabilityInstance` insert: a field schema + nouns. Same interpreter, same `Request` table.

Adding a **new field type** (e.g. `file`) = one handler in `fields/handlers.ts`.

Adding a **new stage type** (e.g. `qualify_score`) = extend types, interpreter, validation, possibly a new LLM port.

